WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.780
Can you please tell us a little bit more
about where you come from

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.880
And maybe a little bit about the Movement for Democratic Change here in Zimbabwe.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.360
Thank you so much, it's a great pleasure to be here

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.800
My name is Douglas Mwonzora, I am the Secretary General of the Movement for Democratic Change.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.220
which is the main opposition party in Zimbabwe, and is hoping to form the next government.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.940
I am a lawyer by profession

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.220
I studied Law at the university of Zimbabwe

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:40.400
I joined politics in 1988, fighting against
the one-party state in Zimbabwe

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.240
I joined quite a couple of different parties 
for purposes, as a young person

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.640
to fight against the one-party state, the dictatorship.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.880
As a result of my fight against the one-party state

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.780
I've been arrested and contained by the 
authorities for 34 times.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.740
Thank God I'm still alive and here to tell the story

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:07.600
I was lucky enough to be appointed the [?] person 
of the constitution making.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.520
by the prime minister then, Morgan Tsvangirai, 
the president of my party

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.760
It is an honour to be asked to write 
a constitution for your country

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.420
When we were doing that project

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:31.300
my eyes were opened: it was not what I want 
that matters to everyone

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.100
As human beings we must be able to accommodate others

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.380
There are other interest groups

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.360
And when you are making a constitution, it's best called a 'concordance for discordant forces in society'.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:56.520
The most important thing for a constitution-maker,
is to put yourself out of the equation.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.120
And look at the best view of society

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.640
and the various interest groups that are there.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:10.300
Knowing that each interest is as important to 
the person who holds it

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.140
as your interest in something else.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.840
Unfortunately after the forging of the new constitution – which was successful –

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.900
before the crafting of the new constitution

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.120
the finishing of the constitution

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.360
it was taken to a referendum

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.280
which was a yes or no vote. Whether the people of Zimbabwe were going to say 'yes' to the constitution

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.380
Historically Zimbabwe has always voted 'no' 
in a referendum

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.600
and everybody thought it would be a question of deja vu

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.680
If the Zimbabweans were going to vote 'yes', 
we were going to make history.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:59.380
And counting down to the referendum, 
my colleagues and I were affraid that

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.860
history would repeat itself.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.020
Fortunately for us, Zimbabweans voted 95%

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.040
for the constitution.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:14.320
And we became the first group of people to do something that went to a referendum

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.760
of which the result was positive.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.420
It was a very humbling experience indeed.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:24.140
Regrettably, I lost my seat

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.920
in the elections of 2013

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.480
Ever since I've been in the opposition

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:37.620
I was from an ordinary member of the party

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:42.240
to being the spokesperson of the party, in 2011

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:47.240
three years later, I was elected 
Secretary General of the movement

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.920
Right now, I've not gone back to teaching, 
I'm running a small private practise

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.140
called 'Mwonzora and Associates', were I've got five partners in the law firm.

